{
    "title": "Wireless Emergency Alerts and the Emergency Alert System; Correction",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 18, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-18/pdf/2025-23337.pdf",
    "summary": "Two federal updates set new timelines for oil and gas oversight and for future multilingual emergency alerts. The Bureau of Land Management is finalizing a deadline of June 22, 2027 for companies to bring their oil and gas \u201cbonds\u201d \u2014 the financial guarantees meant to cover cleanup and other obligations \u2014 up to the required minimums. Separately, the Federal Communications Commission corrected the timetable for multilingual wireless emergency alerts: the key rules will now take effect on June 12, 2028, and some related pieces are delayed without a set date.\n\nFor communities and taxpayers, stronger bonding rules can reduce the risk of abandoned wells and unpaid cleanup costs, helping protect land and public funds. Clear deadlines also give state and local officials certainty about what will be funded if a company walks away. People who rely on phone alerts during fires, storms, or other threats stand to gain when emergency messages are offered in more languages, and the new date gives public safety agencies and mobile providers a firm target to plan toward.\n\nOn the other hand, oil and gas operators \u2014 especially smaller firms or those with older wells \u2014 may face higher costs to raise or replace their bonds by 2027, which could affect marginal projects or lead to consolidation. For residents with limited English proficiency, the delay to 2028 means a longer wait for alerts they can easily understand, and the indefinite pause on some changes creates uncertainty about what will ultimately roll out. Wireless carriers and local agencies will also need to invest time and resources to build, test, and support the new alert options.\n\nIn short, the government is tightening financial safeguards in oil and gas and setting a later, clearer date for multilingual emergency alerts. The changes aim to protect the public and improve safety, while shifting costs and timelines that some companies and communities will feel in the near term.",
